card.statement:
Let $Q$ be a quiver with vertices $1,\dots,t$, and let $\pen\in\{\T,\Aster\}$ be fixed.

- A $^{\pen\!}$unitary representation $\mathcal A=(\mathcal A_{\alpha },\mathcal U_v)$ of $Q$ is given by assigning to each vertex $v$ a $^{\pen}$unitary space $\mathcal U_v$ and to each arrow $\alpha : u \longrightarrow  v$ a linear mapping $\mathcal A_{\alpha}: \mathcal U_u \to \mathcal U_v$.

- Two $^{\pen}$unitary representations $\mathcal A=(\mathcal A_{\alpha },\mathcal U_v)$ and $\mathcal B=(\mathcal B_{\alpha },\mathcal V_v)$ of $Q$ are isometric if there exists a family of isometries (linear bijections that preserve the scalar products) $\varphi_1:\mathcal U_1\to \mathcal V_1,\dots,
\varphi_t:\mathcal U_t\to \mathcal V_t$ such that the diagram [-4mm]

\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\xymatrix{
\mathcal U_u\ar[r]^{\mathcal A_{\alpha} }
\ar[d]_{\varphi_u}
&\mathcal U_v\ar[d]^{\varphi_v}
\\
\mathcal V_u\ar[r]^{\mathcal B_{\alpha} }&
\mathcal V_v}
\end{split}
\end{equation}

is commutative ($\varphi_v\mathcal A_{\alpha }=\mathcal B_{\alpha
}\varphi _u$) for each arrow $\alpha
:u\longrightarrow  v$.
